## Editorial rules

- **One emoji only** — never stack (🍺🎵 is wrong)
- Never use decorative emojis or emojis because they "look fun"
- Icon = **primary reason** someone is going
- If two categories apply, choose the strongest editorial category

Example: brewery hosting live music → `🍺 Desert Eagle Brewing` (not 🍺🎵).
The article may mention live music; the icon is the primary destination.

## Verify before assigning

Do **not** assign an icon until the category has been verified. Never infer from
business name alone — determine what a visitor would actually experience.

Examples:

- "Hurricane Simulator" ≠ Arcade (may be one attraction)
- "Natural Expressions" ≠ Rock Shop (research first, then categorize)

Classification order: verified venue category → experience → `editorialCategory.ts`.
Icons follow verified category via `resolveDiscoveryCategoryIcon`.

## Consistency — icon follows the article everywhere

Same icon on: homepage card · See All · search · Saved · Today's Board ·
article page · related stories.

Store on `KindredArticle.categoryIcon` and `LocalEventCard.categoryIcon`.
Render via `EditorialTitle` / `editorialTitleWithIcon`.

## Not in scope

Today's Masterpiece, The Story of…, Today in History — editorial photography only.
No promotional flyers or scraped event images as icons.

## The Kindred Test

> If someone removed all text except the icon and headline, would the reader
> still immediately understand what kind of place this is?

If not → the category is wrong.

## Goal

Kindred develops its own visual language — like newspaper sections, but for
experience categories. Readers recognize Kindred content at a glance, even
without photographs.
